  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday Night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1138 PM CDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nNear-persistent weather conditions are forecast for the second \nhalf of the workweek. The aforementioned mid/upper-level ridge \naxis is anticipated to remain fixed overhead Thursday and through \nmuch of the day on Friday. Surface winds will remain light and \nwill slowly veer from east to southeast by Friday. Temperatures, \ndespite being noticeably cooler as of late, will remain a handful \nof degrees warmer than seasonal average through Friday and into \nthe upcoming weekend. Expect daytime highs to range from the mid\nto upper 80s (north) to low to mid 90s (south) and nighttime lows\ngenerally in the upper 50s to mid 60s. \n\nUpper-level ridging will begin to flatten and shift east Friday\ninto Friday night as a trough, with embedded shortwaves, begins \nto moves over the Plains. Deterministic models show a weak \nshortwave trough sideswiping the northwest corner of the CWA\nFriday morning and into the afternoon. This will bring low \nprecipitation chances west of US-75 and north of I-44. No impacts\nexpected from this. The residual ridging overhead will keep the \nmain brunt of the trough and moisture plume northwest of the \nforecast area, but as the ridge flattens late Friday night and \ninto Saturday morning, a better chance of showers and \nthunderstorms will occur for portions of eastern OK, with chances \nlikely persisting into the afternoon Saturday. Even better chances\nof precipitation will arrive Saturday evening/night and into \nSunday as the main lobe of energy from the trough moves across \nthe area. The GFS and UKMET have been persistent with a more \nrobust shortwave trough moving across the area Saturday afternoon \nand evening, showing widespread precipitation chances Saturday \nnight into Sunday. Despite shortwave ridging moving over the area \non Monday, low precipitation chances will persist for much of the \narea as a subtle warm frontal boundary moves through the area. \n\nModels and ensembles continue to indicate a vigorous, fall-like \ntrough moving over the Plains on Tuesday, with areawide showers \nand thunderstorms associated with it through the daytime Tuesday. \nThe trough will also bring in a decently strong cold front that is\nexpected to move through sometime Tuesday night into Wednesday \nmorning. Timing of the trough and surface front are subject to \nchange. The front is forecast to drop temperatures near to \nslightly below average by midweek. Better details to come. \n\nMejia",
